## UTILITY CLASSES

The Guava blueprint: `public final class Foo { private Foo() {} }`. Every static utility class follows this exactly.

```java
// WRONG
public class StringUtils {
  public static String normalize(String input) { ... }
}

// RIGHT — modeled directly on Guava's Strings, MoreObjects, etc.
/**
 * Static utility methods for {@link String} manipulation.
 *
 * <p>These methods supplement those already provided by {@link
 * com.google.common.base.Strings} and the JDK.
 */
public final class StringUtils {

  // Private constructor — not instantiable
  private StringUtils() {}

  /**
   * Returns the trimmed, lowercased form of {@code input}, or {@code null}
   * if {@code input} is null or blank after trimming.
   *
   * @param input the string to normalize; may be null
   * @return the normalized string, or {@code null}
   */
  public static @Nullable String normalizeOrNull(@Nullable String input) {
    if (input == null) {
      return null;
    }
    final String trimmed = input.trim();
    return trimmed.isEmpty() ? null : trimmed.toLowerCase(Locale.ROOT);
  }
}
```

Key rules:
- `public final class` — never abstract, never subclassable
- Private no-arg constructor with no body or a comment
- Every method is `public static`
- Null inputs documented with `@Nullable`; non-null documented with `@param`
- No mutable static state ever

