## NULL HANDLING

Guava's approach: be explicit and fail fast. Use `@Nullable` for things that can be null; everything else is non-null by contract.

```java
// Add JSpecify null annotations to your package-info.java
@NullMarked
package com.example.myservice;

import org.jspecify.annotations.NullMarked;
```

```java
// In method signatures:
import org.jspecify.annotations.Nullable;

// Nullable input, nullable output — annotate both
public @Nullable User findByEmail(@Nullable String email) { ... }

// Non-null by contract — nothing needed, NullMarked covers it
public User findById(long id) { ... }
```

### Preconditions at method entry (exactly like Guava does it)

```java
// WRONG — too late, no message, hard to debug
public User createUser(String name, String email) {
  if (name == null) throw new NullPointerException();
  // ...
}

// RIGHT — Guava style: checkNotNull with descriptive message, at the top
public User createUser(String name, String email) {
  checkNotNull(name, "name");
  checkNotNull(email, "email");
  checkArgument(!name.isBlank(), "name must not be blank");
  checkArgument(name.length() <= 100, "name too long: %s", name.length());
  checkArgument(email.contains("@"), "invalid email: %s", email);
  // business logic starts here
}
```

Rules:
- `checkNotNull(ref, "fieldName")` — the message is just the parameter name in Guava
- `checkArgument(expr, "description: %s", value)` — always include the bad value in the message
- `checkState(expr, "description")` — for internal consistency, not caller errors
- Always use `%s` placeholders, never string concatenation inside the check call (it's evaluated eagerly even when the check passes)
- All checks before any computation

---

## PRECONDITIONS: THE THREE-WAY SPLIT

Guava distinguishes these precisely. Follow the same convention:

| Situation | Method |
|---|---|
| Caller passed invalid input | `Preconditions.checkArgument` |
| Object in wrong state for this call | `Preconditions.checkState` |
| Null where null is not allowed | `Preconditions.checkNotNull` |
| Internal invariant that should be impossible to violate | `Verify.verify` |

Never use `Objects.requireNonNull` — use `checkNotNull`. Never throw `RuntimeException` directly — throw the most specific type.

