## JAVADOC

Follow Guava's Javadoc standard exactly.

```java
// WRONG — no Javadoc, or vague Javadoc
public User findById(long id) { ... }

// WRONG — restates what the method name already says
/**
 * Find user by id.
 */
public User findById(long id) { ... }

// RIGHT — Guava style: explains the contract, not the implementation
/**
 * Returns the user with the given {@code id}.
 *
 * <p>If no user exists with {@code id}, throws {@link NoSuchElementException}
 * rather than returning {@code null}.
 *
 * @param id the user's unique database identifier; must be positive
 * @return the user, never {@code null}
 * @throws IllegalArgumentException if {@code id} is not positive
 * @throws NoSuchElementException if no user with {@code id} exists
 */
public User findById(long id) { ... }
```

Rules:
- First sentence: "Returns ...", "Creates ...", "Removes ..." — action verb, object
- `<p>` tag for additional paragraphs after the first
- `{@code value}` for inline code; `{@link ClassName}` or `{@link ClassName#method}` for references
- `@param` — every parameter, describe constraints (e.g. "must be positive", "may be null")
- `@return` — what it is and when it could be null (if ever)
- `@throws` — every declared exception + any important unchecked ones
- Class-level Javadoc: describe what the class is, not how it's implemented; note thread-safety
- Utility class Javadoc: "Static utility methods pertaining to {@link Foo}."
- Do not document `@Override` methods unless you're adding contract details the parent didn't cover

