### NAMING: THE GUAVA CONVENTION

Guava uses a three-part naming convention. Read `PreconditionsTest` — every method follows it:

```
test[MethodName]_[scenario]_[expectedOutcome]
```

Real examples from Guava source:
- `testCheckArgument_simple_success`
- `testCheckArgument_simpleMessage_failure`
- `testCheckArgument_nullMessage_failure`
- `testCheckNotNull_complexMessage_success`
- `testPadStart_noPadding`
- `testPadStart_somePadding`
- `testPadStart_null`
- `testNoOpServiceStopIdempotence`
- `testManualServiceStopWhileStarting`

The name is the specification. If you cannot state the scenario and expected outcome in the method name, the test is probably testing more than one thing.

```java
// WRONG — tells you nothing
@Test
void testFindUser() { ... }

@Test
void test1() { ... }

@Test
void shouldWork() { ... }

// RIGHT — Guava style
@Test
void testFindById_returnsUser_whenUserExists() { ... }

@Test
void testFindById_throwsNoSuchElementException_whenUserDoesNotExist() { ... }

@Test
void testFindById_throwsIllegalArgumentException_whenIdIsZero() { ... }

@Test
void testCreateUser_throwsIllegalArgumentException_whenNameIsBlank() { ... }

@Test
void testCreateUser_throwsDuplicateEmailException_whenEmailAlreadyRegistered() { ... }
```

