### ASSERTIONS: GOOGLE TRUTH

Use Google Truth everywhere. It produces better failure messages than JUnit `assertEquals` and reads more naturally than Hamcrest.

```java
import static com.google.common.truth.Truth.assertThat;
import static org.junit.jupiter.api.Assertions.assertThrows;
```

```java
// For values
assertThat(result).isEqualTo("expected");
assertThat(result).isNull();
assertThat(result).isNotNull();
assertThat(list).isEmpty();
assertThat(list).hasSize(3);
assertThat(list).contains("alice");
assertThat(list).containsExactly("a", "b", "c").inOrder();

// For booleans
assertThat(user.isActive()).isTrue();
assertThat(users).isEmpty();

// isSameInstanceAs vs isEqualTo — Guava is precise about this
// Use isSameInstanceAs when the contract says the SAME object is returned, not just an equal one
assertThat(Strings.padStart("x", 1, '-')).isSameInstanceAs("x"); // no-op case returns same ref
assertThat(Strings.padStart("x", 3, '-')).isEqualTo("--x");       // new string, equality check
```

### ASSERTING ON EXCEPTIONS

Never use `@Test(expected = ...)`. Capture the exception and assert on its message. The message is part of the contract — Guava always tests it.

```java
// WRONG — only checks exception type, not message
@Test(expected = IllegalArgumentException.class)
void testCreate_nullName() {
  service.create(null, "email@example.com");
}

// WRONG — same problem with JUnit 5 style
@Test
void testCreate_nullName() {
  assertThrows(IllegalArgumentException.class,
      () -> service.create(null, "email@example.com"));
  // missing: what was the message? was it the right exception for the right reason?
}

// RIGHT — Guava style: capture the exception, assert on its message
@Test
void testCreate_throwsNullPointerException_whenNameIsNull() {
  final NullPointerException e = assertThrows(NullPointerException.class,
      () -> service.create(null, "email@example.com"));
  assertThat(e).hasMessageThat().isEqualTo("name");
}

@Test
void testCreate_throwsIllegalArgumentException_whenNameIsBlank() {
  final IllegalArgumentException e = assertThrows(IllegalArgumentException.class,
      () -> service.create("   ", "email@example.com"));
  assertThat(e).hasMessageThat().isEqualTo("name must not be blank");
}

@Test
void testCreate_throwsIllegalArgumentException_whenNameIsTooLong() {
  final String longName = "a".repeat(101);
  final IllegalArgumentException e = assertThrows(IllegalArgumentException.class,
      () -> service.create(longName, "email@example.com"));
  // message must contain the bad value — this is the Guava standard
  assertThat(e).hasMessageThat().contains("101");
}
```

---

### FAKES OVER MOCKS

Guava's test suite almost never uses Mockito. Instead they write small, real implementations called fakes. This is a better design for two reasons: fakes enforce the interface contract (they implement the real interface), and they test behavior instead of wiring.

Look at how Guava does it — `RecordingListener` in `AbstractServiceTest` is a fake that records state history, not a mock that verifies call counts. `FakeTicker` in `guava-testlib` is a real `Ticker` implementation whose time you can advance.

```java
// WRONG — mock-heavy test that tests wiring, not behavior
@Test
void testFindById_callsRepository() {
  when(mockRepo.findById(42L)).thenReturn(Optional.of(user));
  final User result = service.findById(42L);
  verify(mockRepo).findById(42L); // testing that YOU CALLED THE REPO, not what came back
  assertThat(result).isEqualTo(user);
}

// RIGHT — write a fake repository, test real behavior
// In test package:
final class FakeUserRepository implements UserRepository {

  private final Map<Long, User> store = new LinkedHashMap<>();
  private long nextId = 1;

  @Override
  public Optional<User> findById(long id) {
    return Optional.ofNullable(store.get(id));
  }

  @Override
  public User save(User user) {
    final User saved = user.withId(nextId++);
    store.put(saved.id(), saved);
    return saved;
  }

  @Override
  public boolean existsByEmail(String email) {
    return store.values().stream().anyMatch(u -> u.email().equals(email));
  }
}

// Test uses the fake:
class DefaultUserServiceTest {

  private final FakeUserRepository repository = new FakeUserRepository();
  private final FakeClock clock = new FakeClock(Instant.parse("2024-01-15T10:00:00Z"));
  private final DefaultUserService service = new DefaultUserService(repository, clock);

  @Test
  void testFindById_returnsUser_whenUserExists() {
    final User saved = repository.save(new User("alice", "alice@example.com"));

    final User result = service.findById(saved.id());

    assertThat(result.name()).isEqualTo("alice");
    assertThat(result.email()).isEqualTo("alice@example.com");
  }
}
```

When is Mockito appropriate? For dependencies you cannot fake cheaply — external HTTP clients, third-party SDKs with complex setup, or when you are specifically testing error-path behavior that a fake cannot easily produce (e.g., a network timeout).

---

### FAKES FOR TIME

Never inject `Instant.now()` or `LocalDate.now()` directly in production code. Inject a `java.time.Clock`. In tests, use a fixed clock. This is the direct equivalent of Guava's `FakeTicker`.

```java
// Production code — takes a Clock
@Service
final class DefaultOrderService implements OrderService {
  private final Clock clock;

  DefaultOrderService(OrderRepository orderRepository, Clock clock) {
    this.orderRepository = checkNotNull(orderRepository, "orderRepository");
    this.clock = checkNotNull(clock, "clock");
  }

  @Override
  public Order create(CreateOrderRequest request) {
    return Order.builder()
        .createdAt(Instant.now(clock)) // uses injected clock
        .build();
  }
}

// Test — fixed clock so createdAt is deterministic
@Test
void testCreate_setsCreatedAtToCurrentTime() {
  final Instant now = Instant.parse("2024-06-01T12:00:00Z");
  final Clock fixedClock = Clock.fixed(now, ZoneOffset.UTC);
  final DefaultOrderService service = new DefaultOrderService(fakeRepo, fixedClock);

  final Order order = service.create(validRequest);

  assertThat(order.createdAt()).isEqualTo(now);
}
```

---

### THE LAZY-EVALUATION ANTI-PATTERN

Guava's `PreconditionsTest` has a specific test for this: the `IGNORE_ME` object that throws `AssertionFailedError` from its `toString()` method. The point is to verify that the error message template is *not* evaluated when the check passes. Apply the same principle: never put expensive computation in test assertions that runs regardless of whether the assertion holds.

---

### UNIT TESTS VS INTEGRATION TESTS

**Unit tests** — no Spring context, no database, no external calls. Fast. Test one class at a time. Use fakes for all collaborators. Run in milliseconds.

```java
// Unit test — no @SpringBootTest, no @ExtendWith(SpringExtension.class)
class DefaultUserServiceTest {
  private final FakeUserRepository repository = new FakeUserRepository();
  private final DefaultUserService service =
      new DefaultUserService(repository, Clock.fixed(...));

  @Test
  void testCreate_persistsUser() { ... }
}
```

**Controller slice tests** — test HTTP mapping, request/response serialization, validation annotations, and exception mapping. Use `@WebMvcTest` + `MockMvc`. Mock only the service layer.

```java
@WebMvcTest(UserController.class)
class UserControllerTest {

  @Autowired MockMvc mockMvc;
  @MockBean UserService userService;

  @Test
  void testFindById_returns200_whenUserExists() throws Exception {
    when(userService.findById(42L)).thenReturn(validUser());

    mockMvc.perform(get("/api/v1/users/42"))
        .andExpect(status().isOk())
        .andExpect(jsonPath("$.name").value("alice"))
        .andExpect(jsonPath("$.email").value("alice@example.com"));
  }

  @Test
  void testFindById_returns404_whenUserDoesNotExist() throws Exception {
    when(userService.findById(99L)).thenThrow(new NoSuchElementException("no user with id: 99"));

    mockMvc.perform(get("/api/v1/users/99"))
        .andExpect(status().isNotFound());
  }

  @Test
  void testCreate_returns400_whenNameIsBlank() throws Exception {
    mockMvc.perform(post("/api/v1/users")
            .contentType(MediaType.APPLICATION_JSON)
            .content("{\"name\":\"\",\"email\":\"alice@example.com\"}"))
        .andExpect(status().isBadRequest());
  }
}
```

**Integration tests** — test the full stack including a real database. Use Testcontainers. Annotate with `@SpringBootTest`. These are slow and few; run them separately from unit tests.

```java
@SpringBootTest
@Testcontainers
@Transactional
class UserServiceIntegrationTest {

  @Container
  static final PostgreSQLContainer<?> POSTGRES =
      new PostgreSQLContainer<>("postgres:16");

  @Autowired UserService userService;

  @Test
  void testCreate_andRetrieve_roundTrip() {
    final User created = userService.create(
        new CreateUserRequest("alice", "alice@example.com"));

    final User found = userService.findById(created.id());

    assertThat(found.name()).isEqualTo("alice");
    assertThat(found.email()).isEqualTo("alice@example.com");
  }
}
```

---

### TEST CLASS STRUCTURE

Mirror the production class structure. One test class per production class. Package mirrors production package (so the test can access package-private classes).

```
src/main/java/com/example/user/
  DefaultUserService.java         ← package-private
  UserController.java             ← package-private

src/test/java/com/example/user/
  DefaultUserServiceTest.java     ← same package, can see package-private
  UserControllerTest.java
  FakeUserRepository.java         ← shared fake, same package
```

The test class organization mirrors Guava's: fields first (service under test + its fakes), then test methods grouped by the method being tested, then private helpers at the bottom.

```java
class DefaultUserServiceTest {

  // --- test doubles ---
  private final FakeUserRepository repository = new FakeUserRepository();
  private final Clock clock = Clock.fixed(Instant.parse("2024-01-01T00:00:00Z"), ZoneOffset.UTC);

  // --- subject under test ---
  private final DefaultUserService service = new DefaultUserService(repository, clock);

  // --- findById tests ---

  @Test
  void testFindById_returnsUser_whenUserExists() { ... }

  @Test
  void testFindById_throwsNoSuchElementException_whenUserDoesNotExist() { ... }

  @Test
  void testFindById_throwsIllegalArgumentException_whenIdIsZero() { ... }

  // --- create tests ---

  @Test
  void testCreate_persistsUserWithGeneratedId() { ... }

  @Test
  void testCreate_throwsNullPointerException_whenRequestIsNull() { ... }

  @Test
  void testCreate_throwsDuplicateEmailException_whenEmailAlreadyExists() { ... }

  // --- private helpers ---

  private static CreateUserRequest validRequest() {
    return new CreateUserRequest("alice", "alice@example.com");
  }
}
```

---

### THE NULL CONTRACT TEST

Every public non-`@Nullable` parameter must throw `NullPointerException` when passed null. Guava uses `NullPointerTester` to enforce this automatically. Do the same for your utility classes.

```java
@Test
void testNullContracts() {
  new NullPointerTester().testAllPublicStaticMethods(StringUtils.class);
}
```

For service classes, write explicit null tests. They are fast and catch regressions:

```java
@Test
void testFindById_throwsNullPointerException_whenNullPassedToNonNullableParam() {
  // checkNotNull in constructor should have already guarded this, but
  // verify the service itself documents and enforces its null contracts
  final NullPointerException e = assertThrows(NullPointerException.class,
      () -> new DefaultUserService(null, clock));
  assertThat(e).hasMessageThat().isEqualTo("userRepository");
}
```

---

### WHAT A GOOD TEST FILE LOOKS LIKE

Count the ratio of assertion lines to setup lines. In a well-written test, assertions dominate. If a single test method has 20 lines of setup and 2 lines of assertion, something is wrong — either the class under test is too hard to construct (constructor injection not used, too many dependencies), or the test is doing too much.

Guava's `StringsTest.testNullToEmpty()` is 3 lines:
```java
public void testNullToEmpty() {
  assertThat(Strings.nullToEmpty(null)).isEqualTo("");
  assertThat(Strings.nullToEmpty("")).isEqualTo("");
  assertThat(Strings.nullToEmpty("a")).isEqualTo("a");
}
```

That is the goal. If your tests look like that, they are good tests.

---

### TESTING PROCESS

When asked to write or evaluate tests for a class:

1. **Read the production class contract.** Every `@throws`, every `@return`, every null annotation is a test case.
2. **Identify the class type.** Utility class, service, controller, value type — each has its appropriate test style.
3. **List the test cases before writing any code.** Name them all. If any name is vague, make it specific before writing the test body.
4. **Write fakes first** if the class under test has dependencies. Do not reach for Mockito until you have decided a fake is impractical.
5. **Write the test, not the setup.** The assertion should be obvious. If you are spending more time on setup than assertion, stop and ask whether the class is testable.
6. **Check coverage by contract, not by line.** Does every documented behavior have a test? Does every `@throws` have a test that checks the exception message?
7. **Delete any test you cannot justify.** If you cannot say what contract it verifies, it should not exist.

